WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.158
Does an extra hour awake mean an extra hour learned?

00:00:03.158 --> 00:00:06.316
Your eyes may stay open while attention struggles.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:09.474
Sleep science examines waking and sleeping together.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:12.632
Authoritative reviews link sleep with learning and memory.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:15.789
The brain does not simply switch off at night.

00:00:15.789 --> 00:00:22.105
Learning begins with engaging with the material.

00:00:22.105 --> 00:00:28.421
Sleep participates in later memory processes.

00:00:28.421 --> 00:00:34.737
Daytime practice and nightly rest work together.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:41.053
This sorting station is a teaching metaphor.

00:00:41.053 --> 00:00:44.211
Sleep does not automatically teach new knowledge.

00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:47.368
One better morning cannot establish causality.

00:00:47.368 --> 00:00:50.526
Sleep needs vary across ages.

00:00:50.526 --> 00:00:56.842
Prioritize revision and leave time for rest.

00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.158
Recall and check your understanding in the morning.

00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:06.316
Seek appropriate support for persistent sleep trouble.

00:01:06.316 --> 00:01:09.474
Resting the body is compatible with learning.

00:01:09.474 --> 00:01:12.632
Space in a plan can support the next step.

00:01:12.632 --> 00:01:15.789
Let day and night do their different work.
